### What happens at the API Gateway?

The client sends an HTTPS request to one public address. The gateway terminates TLS, validates the JWT or OAuth2 token, applies rate limiting, and routes the request to the correct backend by path.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator routes(RouteLocatorBuilder b) {
        return b.routes()
            .route(r -> r.path("/api/v1/transfers/**")
                .filters(f -> f.stripPrefix(1))
                .uri("lb://transfer-service"))
            .route(r -> r.path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                .filters(f -> f.stripPrefix(1))
                .uri("lb://account-service"))
            .build();
    }
}
```

### How does service discovery work?

In a dynamic environment, service instances come and go. A registry like Eureka or Kubernetes DNS holds the current set of healthy instances. The gateway and inter-service clients resolve by logical name, never by hardcoded IP.

### How does the service process the request?

The service receives the request in its controller, validates input, and calls its service layer, which executes business logic against its own database in a local transaction.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final TransferRepository transferRepo;

    public TransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo, TransferRepository transferRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.transferRepo = transferRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public Transfer execute(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account from = accountRepo.findById(fromId).orElseThrow();
        Account to = accountRepo.findById(toId).orElseThrow();
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(from);
        accountRepo.save(to);
        return transferRepo.save(new Transfer(fromId, toId, amount));
    }
}
```

### How do inter-service calls work?

When a service needs data owned by another service, it makes a call guarded by resilience patterns.

#### Synchronous (REST/gRPC)

The transfer service calls the accounts service API and waits. Wrap with a timeout, retries, a circuit breaker, and a bulkhead.

```java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "accountService", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
@Retry(name = "accountService")
@TimeLimiter(name = "accountService")
public Account fetchAccount(String accountId) {
    return restClient.get()
        .uri("http://account-service/api/accounts/{id}", accountId)
        .retrieve()
        .body(Account.class);
}
```

#### Asynchronous (Kafka events)

The service publishes an event and moves on. Consumers react independently.

```java
kafkaTemplate.send("transfer.completed", transferId, event);
```

### How does the response flow back?

The service builds a response, the controller serializes it, and it travels back through the gateway to the client. Non-essential follow-up (notifications, audit) continues asynchronously via events.

### How do logging and tracing work?

When the request enters the gateway, it is tagged with a correlation ID. Every service propagates it in headers. All log lines for one request share that ID.

```java
package com.bank.filter;

import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.web.filter.OncePerRequestFilter;
import java.util.UUID;

public class CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp,
                                    FilterChain chain) throws Exception {
        String corrId = req.getHeader("X-Correlation-Id");
        if (corrId == null) corrId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        MDC.put("correlationId", corrId);
        resp.setHeader("X-Correlation-Id", corrId);
        try { chain.doFilter(req, resp); } finally { MDC.clear(); }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected log output:
[corrId=abc-123] TransferService: Debit SAV2001 50000.00
[corrId=abc-123] AccountClient: Calling account-service for SAV3002
[corrId=abc-123] TransferService: Transfer TXN-789 complete
```

Distributed tracing (Jaeger/Zipkin) shows the request as a waterfall of spans across services, so you can see exactly which hop was slow.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: hardcoded instance address
String url = "http://10.0.1.42:8080/api/accounts/SAV2001";

// CORRECT: logical service name resolved by discovery
String url = "http://account-service/api/accounts/SAV2001";
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Keep services stateless so any instance behind discovery can serve any request.
- Propagate trace and correlation context on every hop; a missed hop creates a blind spot.
- Prefer events for anything that can be eventual to reduce coupling.
- Make retried operations idempotent so a retry after a timeout does not double-charge a customer.
- Design every synchronous dependency assuming it will one day be slow or down.
